# tools/deploy/99-pipettebot.rules — stable /dev/i3mega and /dev/dpette
# symlinks for the pipettebot orchestrator (Pi 3 B+ deploy, issue #126).
#
# VID:PID values below are sourced from docs/hardware.md's "Distinguishing
# the two ports" table (real-hardware measurements), not invented:
#   - Anycubic i3 Mega, older boards (CH340 bridge):        1a86:7523
#   - Anycubic i3 Mega, newer / MARLIN-AI3M (CP2102N):      10c4:ea60
#   - DLAB dPette, all units (CP2102 bridge):                10c4:ea60
#
# IMPORTANT — VID:PID collision: a CP2102N-equipped i3 Mega and the dPette
# enumerate with the SAME VID:PID (10c4:ea60). docs/hardware.md documents
# this explicitly and falls back to the USB chip's serial number
# (ATTRS{serial}) to tell them apart — there is no VID:PID-only rule that
# disambiguates that combination, and this file does not pretend otherwise.
# Two rule tiers follow:
#
#   1. CH340 i3 Mega (1a86:7523) — VID:PID is unique to it, active as-is.
#   2. CP210x i3 Mega / dPette (10c4:ea60) — PLACEHOLDER rules matched on
#      ATTRS{serial}. The placeholder strings below never match a real
#      device, so these two rules are inert until a per-unit operator edits
#      them. Read each device's serial after plugging it in:
#
#        udevadm info --query=property /dev/ttyUSB0 | grep ID_SERIAL_SHORT
#
#      (see docs/sbc-deployment.md "Identifying the two serial ports on the
#      Pi"), then replace <I3MEGA_SERIAL> / <DPETTE_SERIAL> below with the
#      values read back, one per device.
#
# GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660" set the underlying device node's ownership
# explicitly (a symlink has no permissions of its own — access is governed
# by the node it points at). Debian/Raspberry Pi OS's own
# 50-udev-default.rules already assigns tty devices to group `dialout`, so
# this is defense-in-depth / self-documenting rather than a fix for an
# otherwise-root-only node — but it matches orchestrator.service's `User=
# pipettebot` (tools/deploy/bootstrap.sh adds that user to the dialout
# group) explicitly rather than relying on the distro default.
#
# Reload after editing:
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
#
# Verify a rule matches (needs a real device path; not exercisable in a
# container with no udev daemon / sysfs USB tree — see PR notes):
#   udevadm test "$(udevadm info -q path -n /dev/ttyUSB0)"

# --- Tier 1: CH340 i3 Mega — unambiguous VID:PID -------------------------
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", SYMLINK+="i3mega", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660"

# --- Tier 2: CP210x i3 Mega / dPette — same VID:PID, disambiguate by serial
# Per-unit: replace the placeholder ATTRS{serial} value for your hardware.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", ATTRS{serial}=="<I3MEGA_SERIAL>", SYMLINK+="i3mega", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660"
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", ATTRS{serial}=="<DPETTE_SERIAL>", SYMLINK+="dpette", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660"
